ysiology, meaning its head is a deviation-denoting noun (a shortfall, abnormality, or loss) or a deviation qualifier modifies a non-nosological head (a process, anatomical structure, or molecular entity), AND the phrase is framed as a pathological outcome or characteristic of a disease or mutation (e.g., via a causal or characterizing prepositional or verbal frame), THEN label the full noun phrase, including the deviation qualifier and its complement, as DiseaseClass.\n  - **Negative Constraint:** Do NOT apply this rule when the phrase names only an ordinary anatomical, physiological, or molecular entity without a deviation marker; when the deviation term describes a measured value, assay result, or normal or experimental variation rather than a patient's pathological state; when the phrase is a lexicalized disorder name, which is governed by the standard DiseaseClass and SpecificDisease rules; or when the frame points to a causal gene, mutation, or agent. In those cases, do not label the bare head or complement as DiseaseClass.\n  - **Generalization:** The trigger is the relation between a deviation operator and a non-nosological head under a pathological-outcome frame, not any specific word list, so the rule applies to unseen deviation nouns and qualifiers.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 1816, "output_tokens": 2329, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 1875}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}